WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:03.310
- Feedback processes, evaluation cycles up

00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:07.040
or out principal working
in consulting can be tough.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.410
It can be a challenge.

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:11.620
And there is for sure, lots
of pressures attached to it.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:12.950
And a big part of that are

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:15.320
the constant evaluation processes.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.670
Every half a year,

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:18.860
sometimes even every quarter,

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.500
you will get a formal evaluation where

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.950
your performance is being
assessed, where you will be looked

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:26.820
at and people will ask themselves where

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.260
this consultant Heinrich
or whatever your name is.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.130
How did this person perform?

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.810
Welcome to another coffee
break here on my channel

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:34.700
Firm Learning.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.640
My name is Heinrich and
on Firm learning and

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.020
I want to help you to become
successful in the first years

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:40.880
of your career.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.080
In today, we're going to talk

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.720
about the evaluation
processes for consultants.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:46.410
And specifically, I will share

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.190
with you how consultants are evaluated.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:52.530
What are the criteria that
are being applied for your

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:54.420
performance as a consultant assistant.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.880
And of course it's important to understand

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.590
that these processes always
differ slightly firm, by firm.

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:03.180
But in my experience, also
in talking to other friends

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:06.060
working in other firms, I
just know that it really comes

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.630
down to three key criteria
pretty much all firms are using.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.010
So in today's video, I
will explain to you what

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:16.680
these three criteria are,
what you can do to be rated

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.318
well, to be assessed
well in these criteria

00:01:18.318 --> 00:01:20.990
and also to talk about how the performance

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:24.060
of a good consultant
differs from the performance

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.460
of a great consultant.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:28.150
So what you can do to
go from good to great.

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.010
Let's start by talking about the first

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.050
of three key dimensions that are used

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:35.370
by most firms to evaluate your
performance as a consultant.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.830
And these are your analytical abilities.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.470
What does this exactly mean?

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:40.520
What is measured here?

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.460
This means how good you are

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.830
at structuring problems,
at solving problems

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:49.770
and working with data,
getting insights from data.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.260
This is all what's in here.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.040
And especially for junior consultants,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.190
this often means working in Excel, right?

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:58.640
So crunching numbers in Excel,
working with spreadsheets

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.310
getting insights out
of data, but then also

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:03.870
your contribution and team
problem solving sessions.

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:05.720
So if you are sitting
together with your team

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.310
maybe your partners flying in

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:09.550
with your project lead
with other team colleagues

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:12.620
you're debating a problem from
your work from the project.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.740
How well are you to contribute to that?

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:18.120
How much of a value add are
you to solve the problems

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.140
at hand that the team is facing?

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.350
All of this is part of
your analytical abilities

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.310
and all of this would be
measured in this regard.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:27.360
So let's all talk about
how a good consultant

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.320
distinguishes him or herself
from a great consultant

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.340
in regards to these analytical abilities.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:33.970
So a good consultant is doing well

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:35.470
what he or she is being told.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.280
So you might sit together
with a project lead,

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.430
The project lead provides you a data set

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:42.220
and the project lead asks you
to conduct an analysis based

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:43.390
on this data set.

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:45.180
So good consultant would do exactly

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.930
that would prepare a clean

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:50.459
nice analysis would crunch
the numbers, get to a result.

00:02:50.459 --> 00:02:53.870
But the great consultant
would go one step further.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:55.560
The great consultant in the middle

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.610
of the analysis might realize
that the actual question

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:01.903
at hand, cannot really be
answered by these numbers

00:03:01.903 --> 00:03:04.680
that are included in the
dataset, this great consultant

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.780
and would realize that
probably he or she would need

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:10.000
some other data inputs to really
get to a great conclusion.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.030
So instead of now just doing,
just completing this analysis

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:15.583
because this is what the project
lead taught him or herself

00:03:15.583 --> 00:03:17.684
the great consultant would go out,

00:03:17.684 --> 00:03:19.742
potentially talk to another client,

00:03:19.742 --> 00:03:23.750
get this data that would actually
be needed, then integrated

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:27.232
in the analysis and then just
prepare the good final result.

00:03:27.232 --> 00:03:30.570
Then really also answers
the question at hand.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.860
The second evaluation
criteria I want to talk

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:35.200
about are your team abilities.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.610
So what exactly does this
mean, team abilities?

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:41.030
This is how well you
contribute to the overall

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:43.040
success of a team.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.910
Are you just the lonely wolf,
just working for yourself

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:47.350
or are you actually a team player,

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:48.800
also supporting other people,

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.050
supporting the team.

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:52.540
One question always is
to what extent you really

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:54.230
contribute to a positive

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:56.510
and inclusive atmosphere in the team room.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:57.813
Sometimes things can be tough.

00:03:57.813 --> 00:04:00.610
Your team might struggle
with a difficult client.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:03.370
Maybe you need to work late
for several days in a row.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:05.870
And all of these things can really take

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:07.350
a toll on team morale.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.297
Are you then the person
who always complaints?

00:04:09.297 --> 00:04:12.937
adds to all this negativity,
draws everybody down

00:04:12.937 --> 00:04:14.600
or are you the person who

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:17.900
despite difficult circumstances
is able to increase

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:20.158
the team morale, to contribute to create

00:04:20.158 --> 00:04:22.670
an edge to a overall positive atmosphere.

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:25.600
This is always a key
question that also I like to

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.630
use when evaluating
teammates on this criteria.

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:31.000
But then it's also about other
things, how you communicate

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:32.900
how you work together with your teammates.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.170
So if you get some tasks

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:36.378
from your project lead, are you reliable?

00:04:36.378 --> 00:04:38.740
Can you be trusted that you get the done

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:40.210
that you really would be a very

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:42.800
very best to complete the
task as you've been asked

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.278
or maybe you see your teammates
struggling with something?

00:04:45.278 --> 00:04:48.290
Are you taking the time to
sit together with a teammate?

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:50.510
Maybe it's a teammate, which
is a bit more junior than you.

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:51.720
Maybe it's an intern.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:53.450
Are you taking the time
to help that intern to

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:55.036
develop that intern?

00:04:55.036 --> 00:04:57.800
All of these are qualities
which are looked at

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.860
which are considered when
making this decision.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:00.780
So again, let's talk

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:02.840
about what distinguishes a good consultant

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:03.680
from a great consultant.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:06.450
When we talk about these team abilities.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:08.820
A good concise in this regard is reliable.

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:10.367
He or she would get all the things done.

00:05:10.367 --> 00:05:11.980
And then the project lead asked

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:14.130
that consultant to do and the project

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:16.890
lead would ask the consultant
to potentially also pick

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:19.020
up some tasks from another colleague

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.750
even though these tasks
are not necessarily part

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:24.140
of your work you're responsible for,

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:26.730
then you would happily do
that as a good consultant.

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:29.700
The great consultant
would go one step further.

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:30.560
So let's think back

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.380
about the situation where
maybe things are a bit tough,

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:34.860
Team morale is down,

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:36.570
Things are a bit difficult at the moment,

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:40.240
The great consultant would
take initiative would ask him

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.140
or herself, what can
I really do personally

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:43.973
to contribute to that?

00:05:43.973 --> 00:05:46.230
And you, one of the things
that this great consultant

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:48.640
and then could do is
suggest to do a team event.

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:50.110
And this is a great,
concise, and could then

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:52.870
even research potential things
that could be done close

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:55.486
to the office location,
could organize it all

00:05:55.486 --> 00:05:57.190
book it into the calendars

00:05:57.190 --> 00:06:00.110
and make sure that everybody
is having a good time.

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:02.240
These things are always
great because of course

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.046
it's difficult to do this on
top of all your other duties

00:06:05.046 --> 00:06:07.590
also for the project lead,
doing things like that

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:09.180
organizing things like that on top

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:11.170
of everything else can be quite a burden.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:12.520
So everybody will be super happy

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:13.660
if you're taking care of this.

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:15.860
And of course, if you
do this proactively just

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:18.080
taking the initiative, organizing this

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:21.170
this will always be considered
to be a great team player

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:23.640
great contributing to the
overall team atmosphere.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:27.180
The third evaluation criteria
on are your client abilities.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:28.820
So how well are you to work

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:30.870
with and interact with your clients.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:32.840
This can take all kinds of forms.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:35.450
For sure, your ability
to lead client meetings

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:37.290
to need interviews with clients

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:40.860
to communicate things away
to clients will play a role.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:42.540
Your also often new abilities to

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:45.080
create written documents
that are then presented

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:47.580
in front of clients that are
shown in front of clients

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:48.760
goes into that as well.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:50.692
But also your overall ability to

00:06:50.692 --> 00:06:52.876
build a trust-based relationship

00:06:52.876 --> 00:06:55.940
to the extent to which the
client really wants to work

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:57.292
with you because the client sees you

00:06:57.292 --> 00:06:59.395
as a trusted advisor with whom he

00:06:59.395 --> 00:07:03.583
or she would love to talk to
use you as a sparring partner

00:07:03.583 --> 00:07:06.740
get some feedback from
you on ideas, on all kinds

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:08.770
of topics that the client
is currently working on.

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:11.334
And trust me as a project
lead, you notice that,

00:07:11.334 --> 00:07:14.244
you notice if a client just randomly walks

00:07:14.244 --> 00:07:17.140
into your team room and
wants to talk to some

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:19.920
of your consultants because the
client really enjoys talking

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:22.440
to the person just would
love to get an opinion

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:23.273
on that person.

00:07:23.273 --> 00:07:26.480
And here for sure a good junior
consultant would be able to

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:28.410
build a great working relationship

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:31.250
with the consultant where
you can in an effective way

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:34.110
work together, exchange
some ideas, iterate

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:36.691
on some documents, really work
on an end product together

00:07:36.691 --> 00:07:39.870
but the great consultant button
would go one step further.

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:42.910
So not only is this
consultant using the client to

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:44.809
get some information, to get some data

00:07:44.809 --> 00:07:48.210
but the consultant would really
want to build a relationship

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:49.043
with the client

00:07:49.043 --> 00:07:51.600
the relationship where
both parties benefit,

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:53.539
relationship where potentially even after

00:07:53.539 --> 00:07:57.580
the end of the project, both
of you would stay in touch

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:00.270
because especially when you
then get more and more senior

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:03.470
these relationships are for
sure and insanely the important

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:06.177
because these relationships
are the starting position

00:08:06.177 --> 00:08:08.720
of new projects of new client discussions

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:10.920
with them potentially follow-on-projects

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:12.630
might evolve on in the future.

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:13.570
Here's one tip

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:15.560
I got from a project
lead, I worked with one

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:18.730
of my first projects, was
that on every single project

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:21.160
you should try to pick
at least one person, at

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:24.490
least one client with whom
you really enjoy working

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:27.140
and then try to build a
friendship with this client

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:30.690
try to stay in contact right
to this client every six months

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:31.523
or so.

00:08:31.523 --> 00:08:34.950
Stay in contact with this
person, just to build also

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:37.520
your professional network,
because maybe this client

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:40.130
at the time when you work
with this person, isn't even

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:41.868
that senior, isn't even that important

00:08:41.868 --> 00:08:44.640
in the client organization,
but in the same way

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.230
that you progress throughout
your career, the same way often

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:51.030
then clients are progressing
through their careers as well.

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:53.850
So junior client who's junior today, maybe

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:57.780
in 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 years,
once you are a partner is

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:00.020
pretty important pretty
senior client indeed.

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:02.120
And if you start dealing
your professional network

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:04.290
like this early on,
chances are pretty high

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:06.990
that besides all the other
benefits that you've caused also

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:09.050
have by gaining your valuable contact

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:11.383
by expanding your network,
chances are high, that then

00:09:11.383 --> 00:09:14.360
this would really benefit you
also in the long-term goal

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:17.080
of potentially acquiring
new projects for your firm.

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:18.310
I love to hear from you now

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:20.700
what is your experience
with evaluation processes?

